<TopicPage slug="subjects/communication-studies/foundational/communication-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the very first lesson in Communication Studies. You need no prior
    knowledge — no theory, no jargon, nothing. We start from something you have
    already done hundreds of times today: communicated. Then we ask why it so
    often goes wrong.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe the transmission model of communication, and say what it is brilliant at and where it misleads;</li>
    <li>explain in everyday words why "I told them" does not mean "they understood me";</li>
    <li>use the basic tools of semiotics — sign, signifier, signified, denotation, connotation — on a real advert or headline;</li>
    <li>explain what "the medium is the message" claims, and name the affordances that separate one channel from another;</li>
    <li>read a single sentence through all of these lenses at once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Every technical term is defined the moment it appears,
    and each paragraph carries exactly one idea.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a sentence that went wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a workplace scene. A manager tells a team: "the project has been
    deprioritised." The words travel through the air perfectly. Yet one person
    hears "cancelled", another hears "postponed", a third hears "the boss has
    lost interest", and a fourth quietly opens a job site. Nothing failed
    technically — and the outcome is still four different messages.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="walk the four hearings of one sentence" widget="branch" config={comSceneBranch} />
  <p>
    That puzzle is the business of <strong>communication theory</strong>: the
    study of what actually happens when people communicate, not what we hope
    happens. The naive picture — I put my idea into words, the words travel to
    you, and you hold my idea as I meant it — fails constantly. A company
    announcement calms half its staff and alarms the rest. A public health
    message saves lives in one community and hardens resistance in another.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the founding question sounds almost too obvious to ask:
    <em>what actually happens when people communicate?</em> It stops being
    obvious once you try to answer it. The theories in this lesson form a
    spectrum of answers, from the engineers' pipe to the strangest answer of
    all: that our social world is not merely reported by communication — it is
    built out of it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from pipes to constitutive: the model spectrum" widget="equationStep" config={modelsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the spectrum runs from the engineers' pipe to
    the constitutive view, and each model keeps what the last one missed —
    Shannon's channels, negotiated meaning, the medium's own shaping power, and
    finally the claim that communication builds the social world rather than
    reporting on it. The rest of the lesson walks that spectrum one model at a
    time.
  </p>

  <h2>Model one: communication as a pipe</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest model is the <strong>transmission model</strong>, and it is the
    field's most useful villain. It was built in 1948 by Claude Shannon, a
    mathematician at the Bell Telephone Laboratories, and later popularised by
    Warren Weaver. Shannon was not trying to explain people — he was fixing
    telephone signals — but his diagram was so clean that everyone borrowed
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The diagram has five parts. A <strong>source</strong> is the person with
    the idea. An <strong>encoder</strong> turns the idea into signals that can
    travel — like putting a letter into an envelope. A
    <strong>channel</strong> is the road the signal travels: a wire, the air, a
    wifi link. <strong>Noise</strong> is anything that scrambles the signal on
    the way — literally the static on an old phone line. A
    <strong>decoder</strong> turns the signal back into a message, and a
    <strong>receiver</strong> is the person at the other end. Shannon asked one
    precise engineering question: how much information can get through a noisy
    channel, and how do codes beat noise?
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="put Shannon's 1948 assembly line in order" widget="order" config={comPipeOrder} />
  <p>
    For machines it is a triumph: bandwidth, bit rates and error-correcting
    codes descend from it. The trouble starts when the diagram
    is applied to humans without thinking. Telephone messages are fixed in
    advance, and success means the copy matches the original; people are not
    like that. When the engineers' picture creeps into everyday thinking, we
    get the <strong>conduit metaphor</strong>: minds as containers, meanings as
    objects, communication as mailing a parcel. Put the idea in the box, mail
    the box, job done.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our manager's sentence breaks the parcel picture. By the pipe model,
    communication succeeded perfectly: the word "deprioritised" arrived with
    zero noise. And the meaning still went four directions at once. In 1948,
    Harold Lasswell sharpened the model's sociology — "who says what in which
    channel to whom with what effect?" — but the plumbing stayed. A better
    picture was needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the transmission model — source, encoder, channel
    with noise, decoder, receiver — is superb for machines and misleading for
    people, because it treats meaning as a parcel that arrives intact or not
    at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Model two: communication as making meaning together</h2>
  <p>
    The next family of models repaired the pipe by putting the two people back
    in. Wilbur Schramm, working in the 1950s, saw that each of us encodes
    messages through our own <strong>field of experience</strong>: everything
    we have lived through, learned and come to believe. Communication only
    works where two people's fields overlap: meaning is not transferred but
    negotiated across a gap, the way two neighbours who speak related but
    different languages meet in the middle.
  </p>
  <p>
    Charles Osgood and Schramm then bent the model into a circle. While you
    speak, you are also reading your listener's face; while they listen, they
    are already answering with a nod or a frown. Dean Barnlund's
    <strong>transactional models</strong> went further: real conversation is
    simultaneous and multi-channel. Your posture, your silence, where you sit,
    the whole history of the relationship — all of it carries "messages",
    including ones nobody deliberately sent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deepest move belongs to Robert Craig's 1999 paper "Communication
    Theory as a Field". Communication, Craig argued, does not just report on a
    social world that exists beforehand. It <em>produces</em> that world. The
    team's morale, the manager's authority, even the organisation itself exist
    <em>in and through</em> the meetings, the emails and the silences. This is
    the <strong>constitutive view</strong> — "constitutive" means
    making-something-what-it-is. On this view, the manager's sentence did not
    describe the team's status; in that moment it <em>was</em> the team's
    status being made. He also mapped the field's seven traditions —
    rhetorical, semiotic, cybernetic, socio-psychological, sociocultural,
    critical and phenomenological. Do not memorise the list; the point is that
    "what happens when people communicate?" is secretly seven different
    questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> models grew from Shannon's pipe to Schramm's
    overlapping fields, to Barnlund's simultaneous transaction, to Craig's
    claim that communication builds the social world itself. Each model keeps
    what the last one missed.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that communication succeeds when the message "gets through" — and that failure means someone transmitted badly. Under meaning-centred and constitutive models, success is <em>shared interpretation</em>, which is co-produced: the listener's frame, history and expectations are inside the loop. This is why "I told them" is not a defence — transmission is the cheapest part of communication, and often the least of it.</p>

  <h2>Semiotics: meaning lives in codes</h2>
  <p>
    If meaning is negotiated rather than delivered, what is meaning made of?
    That question has its own toolbox: <strong>semiotics</strong>, the study of
    signs. A <strong>sign</strong> is anything that stands for something else —
    a word, a photo, a red light, a thumbs-up.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Swiss linguist Ferdinand de Saussure split every sign into two halves.
    The <strong>signifier</strong> is the physical half: the sound, the mark,
    the image. The <strong>signified</strong> is the mental half: the concept
    the sound or image calls up. And here is the powerful discovery: the link
    between the halves is <strong>arbitrary and conventional</strong> — a
    matter of community agreement, not of nature. Nothing about the sound
    "tree" is leafy, and nothing about a thumbs-up is approval. The agreement
    is visible everywhere: the same gesture flatters in one culture and
    insults in another, and the word for the very same object differs in every
    language on Earth.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the two halves of a sign" widget="cloze" config={comSignCloze} />
  <p>
    Charles Sanders Peirce sorted signs by <em>how</em> they point.
    <strong>Icons</strong> resemble what they show: a photograph, a map.
    <strong>Indices</strong> point by physical connection: smoke means fire, a
    fever means infection. <strong>Symbols</strong> rest on pure agreement:
    words, flags, most of culture. That split still organises media analysis
    today.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each kind with how it refers" widget="match" config={comPeirceMatch} />
  <p>
    Roland Barthes added the layer that turns semiotics into a microscope.
    <strong>Denotation</strong> is the literal sign: this is a photograph of a
    man in a uniform. <strong>Connotation</strong> is the cultural freight
    riding on top: authority, safety, the state. <em>Myth</em> — Barthes'
    technical term, not "a false story" — is what happens when connotations
    harden into truths that feel natural.
  </p>
  <p>
    Advertising is where you can watch the machinery running. The
    product's signifier — a car — is deliberately chained to connotations the
    object itself does not possess: freedom, desirability, responsibility. The
    association is manufactured, then renewed by repetition until it feels
    natural. Logos, news photographs and political words like "reform" run on
    the same machine. This is also how the manager's word did its
    four-directional work: one stable signifier, with each listener activating
    a different code. Codes, not objects, carry the payload.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file each layer under the tool that reads it" widget="sort-buckets" config={comLayerSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a sign pairs a physical signifier with a mental
    signified, and the pairing is agreed rather than natural. Denotation is the
    literal sign; connotation is the cultural freight on top; myth is freight
    that has hardened into common sense.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a sign's meaning is natural or universal — that a thumbs-up, a red light or a word simply "is" what it means. Every signifier–signified pairing is learned from a community, which is why gestures, colours and words flip their meanings across cultures and eras; when an advert "just feels" trustworthy, that feeling is a connotation someone built.</p>

  <h2>Medium and message: the form is an argument</h2>
  <p>
    There is a third ingredient besides sender and sign: the channel itself.
    Marshall McLuhan's 1964 provocation — <em>the medium is the message</em> —
    claims that the <em>form</em> of a medium reshapes what communication can
    be, independently of any particular content. McLuhan was radicalising his
    teacher Harold Innis, who argued that media have biases: stone lasts but
    cannot travel, papyrus travels but fades, and empires rise on their media.
    McLuhan's version: print fostered linear argument and private reading;
    television fostered spectacle and simultaneity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Walter Ong's history of writing gives the concrete version. Cultures
    without writing remember and argue differently — formulaic, rhythmic, tied
    to particular situations — because a spoken-only medium shapes the habits
    of thought. The toolkit for these differences is
    <strong>affordances</strong>: what a medium makes easy or hard. A spoken
    conversation vanishes as it happens; a letter lasts a century; a social
    media post is <em>replicable</em> — it can be screenshotted in seconds —
    and scalable, searchable and editable besides.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag the divider across the affordances" widget="compare" config={comAffordCompare} />
  <p>
    The manager's sentence proves the point. Said aloud in a meeting, it could
    be walked back. Sent as an email, it becomes a record. Leaked as a
    screenshot, it becomes permanent and context-free. Same words; three
    different events.
  </p>
  <p>
    One honest caveat keeps the theory scientific.
    <strong>Technological determinism</strong> is the claim that media
    <em>cause</em> social forms — and as a law of history it overstates the
    case. Societies shape their media as much as media shape societies; the
    same television technology served totalitarian and democratic projects
    alike. McLuhan's aphorism works best as a strong hypothesis about
    affordances, not an iron law.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="true or false — how far the provocation goes" widget="judge" config={comDeterminJudge} />
  <p>
    A century of <strong>effects research</strong> — what media do to
    audiences — has swung through answers. The earliest guess was the
    "hypodermic needle": messages injected into passive audiences. Then came
    the "limited effects" era: studies found people mostly talk each other out
    of persuasion. Today's settled middle ground includes
    <strong>agenda setting</strong> — Maxwell McCombs and Donald Shaw showed in
    1972 that media may not tell us what to think, but they powerfully tell us
    what to think <em>about</em> — alongside <strong>framing</strong> (how the
    telling is done), <strong>cultivation</strong> (slow, long-term shaping of
    assumptions) and Elisabeth Noelle-Neumann's <strong>spiral of
    silence</strong>: people go quiet when they believe their opinion is
    unpopular, and the silence makes it look still more unpopular. Later
    lessons in this course trace that lineage in detail.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="slide to the year of the settled middle ground" widget="place" config={comAgendaPlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the medium's form is itself an argument (McLuhan);
    its affordances — persistence, replicability, scalability, searchability,
    editability — are the practical difference between channels; and effects
    research has settled on influence rather than injection.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "the medium is the message" means content does not matter, or that technology alone decides what a society becomes. McLuhan's claim is narrower: a medium's form reshapes communication <em>independently of</em> any particular content — and societies shape their media back, so the causation runs in both directions.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>An email arrives without a single transmission error, yet half the staff panic. Which models explain this, and why does the transmission model not?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between a sign's denotation and its connotation? Give one example of each from advertising.</li>
    <li>The same bad news is delivered in a meeting and later leaks as a screenshot. Why are these two different communication events, even though the words are identical?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) The meaning-centred and constitutive models:
    each reader decoded the word through their own field of experience, so
    meaning was negotiated, not delivered — the transmission model only asks
    whether the signal arrived, and it did.
    (2) Denotation is the literal sign ("a photograph of a car"); connotation
    is the cultural freight on top ("freedom, status"). (3) The channels have
    different affordances: a meeting is ephemeral and can be walked back; a
    screenshot is permanent, replicable and stripped of context.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The transmission model (Shannon–Weaver, 1948) is superb for machines and misleading for people: meaning is not a parcel that arrives intact.</li>
    <li>Meaning-centred models treat communication as negotiated across overlapping fields of experience — "I told them" does not mean "they understood me".</li>
    <li>The constitutive view: identities, organisations and relationships exist in and through communication, not before it.</li>
    <li>A sign pairs a signifier with a signified, and the pairing is arbitrary and conventional — agreed by a community, not natural.</li>
    <li>Denotation is the literal sign; connotation is the cultural freight on top; myth is connotation hardened into common sense.</li>
    <li>The medium is the message: a channel's affordances — persistence, replicability, scalability, searchability, editability — make the same words a different event.</li>
    <li>Effects research has settled on influence rather than injection: agenda setting, framing, cultivation and the spiral of silence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson built the field's map; the next lessons travel it. The effects
    lineage sketched above — agenda setting, framing, cultivation, the spiral of
    silence — is traced in detail by later lessons in this course, as is Stuart
    Hall's encoding/decoding bridge from semiotics to audiences and the algorithmic
    turn now pressing on the field's oldest distinctions. Keep the spectrum handy:
    every case you meet will be one of four questions — delivery, meaning, form or
    constitution.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One worked analysis shows the spectrum at work on a single message. Take a government's pandemic slogan — "stay home, protect the
    NHS, save lives", the United Kingdom's 2020 formulation and one of the
    century's most-repeated campaigns. Read as <em>transmission</em>, the
    questions are reach and fidelity: did the words arrive, and did people
    remember them? (Yes, and yes.) Read as <em>meaning-centred</em>, the
    questions multiply: what does "stay home" mean to a flat-dweller with
    children, to a gig worker choosing between rent and quarantine, to someone
    whose home is not safe? Read <em>semiotically</em>, the three-part slogan
    binds three signifiers — private act, national institution, universal
    value — into a chain that quietly shifts responsibility toward the
    individual; critics read the chain, supporters read solidarity. Read as
    <em>medium</em>: the slogan's rhythm was engineered for broadcast
    repetition, then detonated by social media's remix affordances — parodied,
    photoshopped, protested. Read as <em>constitutive</em>: the slogan did not
    describe a shared national effort — it was one of the practices
    <em>making</em> that effort, and its differing receptions marked the
    communities it was constituting differently. No single tradition owns the
    case; the spectrum is the analysis, and the analyst's first professional
    decision is always which question — delivery, meaning, form or
    constitution — the case at hand is actually about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three threads carry this foundation into current research.
    <strong>Encoding/decoding</strong> (Stuart Hall, 1973) supplies the bridge
    from semiotics to audiences: producers <em>encode</em> preferred meanings,
    but audiences may read dominantly, negotiate, or read
    <em>oppositionally</em> — consent, irony or hostility from the same
    broadcast — so effects and meanings must be studied as encounters, not
    deliveries. <strong>The constitutive programme</strong>
    has become organisational theory's language: organisations "exist" as
    communication — meetings, memos, onboarding rituals, the org chart — and
    identity, personal and corporate, is performed in interaction; the
    manager's sentence did not describe the team's status, it <em>was</em> the
    team's status being made. <strong>The algorithmic turn</strong> is the
    live frontier: when a recommender system selects which messages reach
    whom, "the channel" acquires an agency Shannon never modelled — virality,
    filter bubbles, misinformation and AI-generated content all press on the
    field's oldest distinctions (source, message, channel, noise) and
    sometimes dissolve them. The seven traditions each grab a piece of that
    question — systems, codes, effects, communities, power, experience,
    influence — and the founding spectrum, from pipe to constitutive, is
    still the field's best map of which piece each theory holds.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCA learning outcomes; Shannon &amp; Weaver, <em>The Mathematical Theory of Communication</em> (1948); McLuhan, <em>Understanding Media</em> (1964); Craig, "Communication Theory as a Field" (1999). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>

